## Research summary

**Library:** `org.flywaydb:flyway-sqlserver:13.5.0` — a thin driver module (`flyway-core` + Lombok only; no other runtime deps per its POM) that adds SQL Server support to Flyway.

**Existing scaffold:** `tests/src/org.flywaydb/flyway-sqlserver/11.8.1/` already covers this shape — it starts `mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/server:2022-CU14-ubuntu-22.04` via Docker in `@BeforeAll` (image is already allow-listed at `tests/tck-build-logic/.../Dockerfile-mssql_server` with that exact tag), depends on `com.microsoft.sqlserver:mssql-jdbc:12.2.0.jre11` for the JDBC driver, and runs a plain `Flyway.migrate()` against it. This exact scaffold (same `test-version = 11.8.1`) already covers `12.3.0` through `13.4.0` per `metadata/org.flywaydb/flyway-sqlserver/index.json`, spanning a major version bump (12→13) without needing scaffold changes — strong evidence the harness generalizes across this range.

**13.5.0 release notes** (Red Gate release notes page) show only: `jackson-databind` became optional in `flyway-core`, JSON support moved to a new `flyway-core-utilities` module, and minor transitive library bumps. None of this is exercised by the existing test — it never touches JSON serialization or `MigrateResult` reporting, only `migrate().success`/`migrationsExecuted`. `flyway-core`'s own 13.5.0 test scaffold (already in-repo) likewise needed no `flyway-core-utilities` dependency.

The issue's log excerpt is truncated before any actual error/exception line (it cuts off mid successful-looking analysis/build summary), so there's no concrete failing operation to trace into library source — per the supportability protocol, that uncertainty alone rules out `skip_unsupported`.

No evidence surfaced for a new Docker image, new Maven dependency, or new environment/system property beyond what the generated scaffold (copied from `11.8.1`, reusing the same pinned `mssql-jdbc` and `mssql/server` image) already provides.

